LOCATION:SIDNEY AND LOIS ESKENAZI MUSEUM OF ART
GEO:39.169144;-86.519739
SUMMARY:Exhibition Closing Celebration for Mulyana: Vital Ecosystems
DESCRIPTION:This unique event invites visitors of all ages to experience 
 the exhibition through sound\, storytelling\, and hands-on creativity.Eve
 nt Schedule2–2:30 p.m. | Musical PerformanceFirst-floor gallery  Immers
 e yourself in Wave Decay – Open Ocean\, a live string quartet performan
 ce featuring music by Timothy Reinholz. Part of the larger Wave Decay ser
 ies\, the work transforms real-time ocean buoy data into sound\, mapping 
 wave motion to pitch\, rhythm\, and dynamics to create an evolving musica
 l landscape. The data follows the migratory route of a long‑distance sh
 orebird across the Pacific\, echoing the oceanic themes and interconnecte
 d ecosystems explored in the Mulyana&#160\;exhibition.3–4 p.m. | Artist
  Talk:&#160\; Hearing Data: Environmental SonificationMartin Commons  Com
 poser Timothy Reinholz will share insights into his creative practice\, e
 xploring how climate and environmental data inform his work and how compl
 ex ecological issues can be translated into powerful musical experiences.
 Throughout the AfternoonLight refreshments  Craft activities for all ages
 \, inspired by the exhibition's themes of marine life\, care\, and interc
 onnected ecosystems    Come reflect\, create\, listen\, and celebrate &#1
 60\;Mulyana: Vital Ecosystems\, closing on June 28th.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  This unique event invites visitors of a
 ll ages to experience the exhibition through sound\, storytelling\, and h
 ands-on creativity.</p><p><strong>Event Schedule</strong></p><p><strong>2
 –2:30 p.m. | Musical Performance</strong><br /><em>First-floor gallery<
 /em></p><p>  Immerse yourself in <i>Wave Decay – Open Ocean</i>\, a liv
 e string quartet performance featuring music by Timothy Reinholz. Part of
  the larger <i>Wave Decay</i> series\, the work transforms real-time ocea
 n buoy data into sound\, mapping wave motion to pitch\, rhythm\, and dyna
 mics to create an evolving musical landscape. The data follows the migrat
 ory route of a long‑distance shorebird across the Pacific\, echoing the
  oceanic themes and interconnected ecosystems explored in the <em>Mulyana
 &#160\;</em>exhibition.</p><p><strong>3–4 p.m. | Artist Talk:&#160\; He
 aring Data: Environmental Sonification</strong></p><p><em>Martin Commons<
 /em><br />  Composer Timothy Reinholz will share insights into his creati
 ve practice\, exploring how climate and environmental data inform his wor
 k and how complex ecological issues can be translated into powerful music
 al experiences.</p><p><strong>Throughout the Afternoon</strong></p><ul><l
 i>Light refreshments  </li><li>Craft activities for all ages\, inspired b
 y the exhibition's themes of marine life\, care\, and interconnected ecos
 ystems  </li></ul><p>  Come reflect\, create\, listen\, and celebrate &#1
 60\;<em>Mulyana: Vital Ecosystems\,</em> closing on June 28th.</p>
